<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  App-level privacy manifest for Liberated Bread.

  The app collects no data and tracks no one: no advertising SDK, no analytics,
  no identifiers leave the device, and nothing about what the user controls is
  reported anywhere.

  It is not, however, offline-only, and the previous wording here ("its only
  outbound request is an anonymous GET for the banner config") was wrong. Every
  connection beyond the user's own devices, so that this list and the App
  Privacy answers can be checked against the code:

    * the banner config GET (lib/core/constants.dart) — anonymous;
    * a device-profile pack download, if the user installs one
      (lib/services/spec_pack_service.dart) — from GitHub or a URL they enter;
    * the user's own Home Assistant server, if they configure it;
    * a one-time sign-in to iRobot's cloud IF the user chooses the account
      route for a robot vacuum rather than typing its details in by hand
      (lib/services/irobot_cloud_service.dart) — this posts their vendor
      account credentials to the vendor to read the robot's LOCAL password,
      which is what makes local control possible afterwards. Disclosed on the
      form that asks for them.

  None of these is a collection of data BY this app, which is why
  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes stays empty; they are listed because a privacy
  manifest that quietly understates a network surface is worse than one that
  does not mention it.

  Required-reason APIs (NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes). Most are declared in the
  bundled plugins' OWN privacy manifests, which travel in their frameworks (the
  Podfile uses use_frameworks!): Flutter, flutter_secure_storage,
  shared_preferences_foundation, url_launcher_ios and permission_handler_apple
  each ship one.

  The app-level list here covers what those do NOT: an embedded binary with no
  manifest of its own. That is liberated_bread_core.framework, the Rust core.
  cargokit builds it from a bare podspec (rust_builder/ios/*.podspec) with no
  Resources, and `nm -u` on the built framework lists _stat, _fstat, _lstat and
  _fstatat — all four in Apple's NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp
  list. They come in through Rust std's unix fs layer and the `backtrace`
  crate that flutter_rust_bridge depends on, NOT from any deliberate file I/O
  in rust/src (the only std::fs use there is test-only). C617.1 is the
  container-files reason, the same one Flutter declares for the same symbols.

  This matters because it is an automated gate, not a review opinion: App Store
  Connect scans every Mach-O in the bundle on upload and answers a missing
  declaration with ITMS-91053, before a human sees the build. Without the entry
  below, docs/APP_STORE_SUBMISSION.md Step 5 cannot complete.

  ios/Runner/AppDelegate.swift itself still uses none. If the Rust core ever
  grows real file I/O, or a new dependency pulls in another required-reason
  category, re-run `nm -u` on the release framework and extend this list —
  test/platform/ios_privacy_manifest_test.dart is what fails if you don't.

  This file IS a member of the "Runner" target's Copy Bundle Resources build
  phase — wired directly in ios/Runner.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj (search that
  file for PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy: a PBXFileReference, a PBXBuildFile, an entry
  in the Runner group, and an entry in the Resources build phase). So it ships
  in the app bundle. If you regenerate the project, re-add it the same way (in
  Xcode: drag into the Runner group and tick the Runner target).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
